>at 100, for a encryption utility that the public has no idea why they  
>"need" it's dead, red taped, chapter 11ville.  Pitty too, since it  
>needs to be put out in mass quantities.
>
>-monty

Note, however, that the announcement says that the freeware version of PGP
will still be available - in addition, compatability between the freeware
version and the commercial version will be essential for it to be a
world-wide standard, since the commercial PGP will be restricted to the US
and Canada.

They specifically mention that they're aiming for the corporate market, where
PGP has been little used due to it's 'illegality'. Obviously, at the $100 price,
many individuals are simply going to keep the freeware PGP and accept the limited
chance that PKP will send in the cops to bust them, whereas corporations who
have considered the use of PGP in the past will now be able to do so without
the rather greater risk that the same might happen to them.

Lastly, of course, the simple existence of a commercial version of PGP helps
to legitimize the freeware users - currently if you have a public key on a
keyserver it is pretty certain that you've got a copy of the unlicensed PGP
and are willing to use it, whereas once the licensed version is released, who
knows who is licensed and who isn't ?


