Subject: Plugging Game Computers into the Net

> > The obvious reason to plug your game computer into the internet isn't
> > e-mail, really.
> 
> You might be right... my concern when thinking about this is the
> message latency in a network of this type.  I would think that
> there could be considerable delays in packet delivery, on the
> order of seconds even.  If somebody disagrees with this for a
> technical reason, please speak up... it would be useful to try
> to pin down how the net of the future will perform.

This is one of the reasons why I don't see the Internet as the end-all
of telecommunications, but rather just one component which will combine
later with others. Today, how do we accomplish this? Modems, of course.
Many games are modem-compatible, and at 9600 baud (most I know of
max out at this, although I'm sure others are working with up to 14.4K
and v.42 compression), they run quite respectably. Most BBSes with multi-
player, live games also work fairly well in real-time as well. These
applications simply aren't going to migrate to larger networks or
services until the network throughput and processing power are increased.

> > Its MUDs. Or some more visual multi-player, multi user game.
> 
> I'm curious about the packet charge.  If it costs a penny
> to deliver a 1K email message, that's probably okay.  But
> if it costs too much per play to play a game (say, more than
> a nickel a minute) the market will be restricted.
> 
> Of course, different games have different bandwidths...
> consider an ICS (internet chess server, available for quite
> a while) client.  It won't really need to send more than a
> couple of bits per second.

I guess here we have to ask ourselves, how do the phone companies get
away with flat monthly rates for local service? I can call all the
computers I want in my local calling area and transfer all I want for
no extra charge. Is it the infrastructure that allows them to offer
the service for so cheap? Is it that long-distance calls offset losses
taken in local calling? Does that $3-$4 you pay for unlimited local 
calls really cover it? All I know is, if the phone company can do it,
there is certainly promise for future computer networks.

<much deleted about various cartridges.

I still think the whole idea of cartridges for internet access on a
game machine is kind of silly. If you're going to want internet access,
you're _not_ going to be too happy if you have to put one cartridge in
for email, one for every game you play, another for news, another for
stock quotes, and so on and so on. That's why we all have PC's, and not
Atari 2600's on our desks. An all-purpose cartridge which can store various
programs and such, you propose? If we're going to go that far, you may as
well just start over with a _real_ computer anyway. It's kind of like
adding a keyboard and disk drive to your game machine. To go that far and
then still be limited by the specific software for that system, is absurd.
Better to get an all-purpose, standard computer, like a PC, Mac or Amiga.

I really _can_ appreciate the concept of wanting to get folks exposed to
the internet via the simplest means possible. I just think that if it is
going to happen, it will either be computer oriented, or TV oriented. This
means, instead of focusing on those people who have Segas or Nintendos, 
why not just design your product for people who own a TV? Much larger
market, and you are no longer saddled with the constraints of the game
system design. Design a board, put a box around it, maybe add a PCMCIA
port or something, and connections for the network (phone, network, cable,
whatever), and away you go. I _think_ am describing what the industry is
actually moving towards. The point? Focus on the game systems, and you'll
find yourself outpaced by those who had a broader strategic direction in
mind.

