Date: Thu, 26 Aug 93 21:59:49 PDT
From: thamilto@pcocd2.intel.com (Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~)
Subject: Plugging Game Computers into the Net

> Perhaps not with ISDN technology which the phone companies are pushing.
> Any idea on the data transfer rate required for real-time interactive
> games?

I personally think that current modem communications is well ahead of the
software needs for interactive games. With a 14.4 V.42 modem, you can
_average_ about 38K bps. For most interactive games, you're not transferring
raw data, but rather tokens or command sequences - a game protocol if you will.
Each end has all the graphics and processing power it needs to handle all the
interpretation of that data, converting it into whatever visual form is 
required by the game. Sure, we'll want to send more in the future, but I
am confident the technology will keep pace. First you have to come up with
the applications, though.

If you doubt what can be transferred over a modem, try it sometime. I once
developed an X-like protocol for Commodore 64's running at 300bps. I was
tinkering with writing some 2-player modem games. Only got as far as some
interesting BBS-hosted games before my interests moved me elsewhere. Anyway,
at 300bps I was able to provide a _great_ deal of interactive, real-time
communications. The fewer the resources you have, the better you get at
optimizing them. I'd very much like to get into doing something like that
again, sometime, but with 14.4 modems ... :-)

